WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.640
Hi there.

9
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.240
Hey.

10
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.400
We are about to start down a very exciting road.

11
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.460
We are moving on.

12
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.420
Part one.

13
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.200
Part one.

14
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.020
And then part two.

15
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.960
It's a big episode.

16
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.940
It really is.

17
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:36.960
And we'll tell you why.

18
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.660
Should we get into it?

19
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:39.540
We better.

20
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:40.540
We have a lot to cover.

21
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:47.300
It is Season 9, Episode 16, written by Graham Wagner and directed by Jon Favreau.

22
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:47.980
Yeah.

23
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.480
Here's your summary.

24
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.660
An already grieving Andy flips out when he learns about Aaron and Pete's new relationship.

25
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.000
Pam has a job interview in Philly.

26
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.360
And the boss's personality is eerily familiar.

27
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.400
Meanwhile, Dwight enlists Angela for help bathing his elderly, ailing aunt.

28
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.500
You know, I know Dwight says that Aunt Shirley is ailing, but she looked pretty sturdy.

29
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.620
Very hardy.

30
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.940
Very hardy to me.

31
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.800
Certainly her personality.

32
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:17.580
Uh-huh.

33
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:18.560
Yes.

34
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:19.660
All right.

35
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.300
Fast fact number one.

36
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.920
Jon Favreau as our director.

37
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.080
You guys know Jon Favreau.

38
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.940
He is a prolific actor, writer, and director.

39
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.400
He wrote and starred in the movie Swingers, which is where I was introduced to him.

40
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.720
Oh, my gosh.

41
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.420
I think our whole generation was that you just wanted to be in that movie.

42
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.840
You were like, oh, I've been there and I've done that, but it didn't look half as cool.

43
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:42.740
I know.

44
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.200
They go to this swing club called the Derby, which was a real place in Los Angeles.

45
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.520
And, you know, I started going to the Derby after I saw that movie.

46
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:51.980
I'd never been.

47
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:52.980
So many people did.

48
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:53.660
I know.

49
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.340
And I would swing dance and it was so fun.

50
00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:57.660
Oh, my gosh.

51
00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:58.780
I think it closed down.

52
00:01:59.140 --> 00:01:59.980
The Derby.

53
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:00.980
Yeah.

54
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:01.700
I know.

55
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:09.440
Anyway, Jon Favreau has also directed Iron Man, Iron Man 2, The Jungle Book, and he directed

56
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.500
and starred in one of my personal favorite movies that I've watched multiple times, Chef.

57
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.020
Have you seen this movie Chef?

58
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:16.580
No.

59
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:17.660
Oh, my God.

60
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:22.900
He plays a chef and he's at a fancy restaurant and he has a big meltdown.

61
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:29.520
And so his only option is to then buy a food truck and sell food out of the food truck.

62
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.900
This kind of sounds like that show The Bear.

63
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.000
Chef at a fancy place.

64
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.080
Oh, no.

65
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.840
Like, it has to go to a smaller place to cook.

66
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.320
I'm doing a horrible summary of it.

67
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:42.860
Yeah.

68
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.620
I don't watch The Bear, so I don't know, but I watch Chef multiple times.

69
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.380
And I did not know this fact.

70
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.560
Did you know that he directed the Will Ferrell Christmas movie Elf?

71
00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:55.280
Really?

72
00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.620
Jon Favreau directed Elf.

73
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.080
God, I love Elf.

74
00:02:59.220 --> 00:02:59.520
Yeah.

75
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.640
I've seen that movie many times.

76
00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.500
Well, I know another thing you've seen that he is like the executive producer creator of,

77
00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.760
which is The Mandalorian.

78
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:08.460
You betcha.

79
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:09.860
Oh, my gosh.

80
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:10.600
The Mandalorian.

81
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.120
And, you know, one of the coolest things for y'all that watch it,

82
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.620
don't you just love, like, the drawings of the scenes are so beautiful,

83
00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.580
how they do that in the credits?

84
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:21.720
I wouldn't know.

85
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:22.340
Oh, Jenna.

86
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:23.260
Okay.

87
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:24.420
All right.

88
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.120
And we've got a couple of really fun crossover connections.

89
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.920
He was in the movie The Replacements with David Denman and Keanu Reeves.

90
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:33.540
Yeah.

91
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:34.740
Office Lady's favorite.

92
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.620
And he has appeared on Monk.

93
00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.300
I have a crossover with Jon Favreau.

94
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:40.680
You do.

95
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:41.780
You have an acting crossover.

96
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.920
You know, we talked a lot about what it was like to work with Jon during our interview

97
00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.040
with Jake Lacey because this was very memorable for all of us.

98
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:50.880
It really was.

99
00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.780
You know, we were all so excited to work with him because we were all fans of his work.

100
00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.160
And he's a real, like, actor's actor.

101
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.440
He's a director that, like, he gets in the scene with you and helps you figure it out.

102
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:04.600
And we love that.

103
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.740
But he's also just really easy to talk to.

104
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.140
I have such fond memories of hanging out with him when we were filming at Aunt Shirley's house.

105
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.200
We had to wait for it to get dark.

106
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.440
We had a little bit of downtime between scenes because we did all of those scenes on one day.

107
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.140
And it has to end with that scene on the front porch with Dwight and Angela, right?

108
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.340
So we're sitting on the back porch of this house.

109
00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:36.320
And he and I just ended up talking about improv and our love of it and of being in the moment.

110
00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.940
And then different improv shows we did.

111
00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:42.000
We did that geeky thing that improv actors do where they talk about bits they did.

112
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.260
But no one else can really get them because it happened in the moment.

113
00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:50.400
You know, Angela, I loved hearing you talk about him because that was exactly my same experience of him.

114
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:58.400
And I find that a lot of times you have a director that's either, like, really good with actors or maybe that's really good on the technical side.

115
00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.880
And John is one of those people who shines in both spaces.

116
00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.340
I asked Steve Burgess, how did we get him?

117
00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.400
How did we get Jon Favreau?

118
00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:07.880
Yeah.

119
00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:15.860
And Steve said, you know, we went out to a lot of what he called journeymen directors, who we also knew to be fans of The Office.

120
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.440
And Jon Favreau was a fan and the dates worked out.

121
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.140
Steve said he's pretty much the busiest guy in showbiz and still is.

122
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:30.960
But Steve Burgess had worked with him before and just knew that he is always great to work with and that this was no exception.

123
00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:41.880
I know that our crew loved Jon because I was trading emails with Dave Rogers, who said that while Claire edited this episode, everyone in post-production loved Jon.

124
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.440
They were so thrilled that he came to work on the show.

125
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.420
Dave said he got Jon Favreau to sign his Swingers script.

126
00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:50.400
Dave, I love that.

127
00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.680
Dave is such a fan of movie and film.

128
00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:55.500
And that is so cute.

129
00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:56.100
I love that.

130
00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.080
I know.

131
00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:03.180
Dave said that all of post-production had gone to see the first Avengers together in the theater.

132
00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:08.800
So this was very exciting to have Jon in their area.

133
00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:10.860
I bet they were all so tickled.

134
00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:12.000
We were all so tickled.

135
00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:12.820
All so tickled.

136
00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.740
And you know, Angela, we've shared this before, but this episode of Moving On was originally supposed to be just one episode.

137
00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.960
It was not going to be a two-parter,

138
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:30.980
but Greg loved everything that Jon turned in so much that he went back to the network and asked them to make it two episodes so he could use all the footage.

139
00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.140
All right.

140
00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:33.660
Are you ready for Fast Fact No.

141
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:34.040
2?

142
00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:35.180
Yes, I am.

143
00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.640
Well, we have two amazing guest stars in this episode.

144
00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:40.500
Fast Fact No.

145
00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.660
2 is all about Bob Odenkirk as Mark.

146
00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.420
I enjoyed these scenes so much.

147
00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:46.980
Me too.

148
00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:53.980
I actually re-watched them this morning before we came in because I wanted to learn the song he sings.

149
00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:55.440
You did?

150
00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:55.860
Yeah.

151
00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.460
That he sings to Pam?

152
00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:57.940
Yes.

153
00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.120
Okay, go on.

154
00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:07.960
So, you know, in Season 9, when we were sort of discussing this storyline of Pam eventually trying to get a job in Philly,

155
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:16.920
Greg thought it would be really funny if the office that she interviews at in Philly is like an alternate Dunder Mifflin universe.

156
00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.440
So, like, while Jim's life and career in Philly is like taking him to all new places.

157
00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.840
Yeah, it's like sleek and shiny and there's a foosball table and fancy conference room.

158
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.260
This looks like a step back, right?

159
00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:30.940
Yes.

160
00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.940
Right back to the beginning of her time at Dunder Mifflin.

161
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.280
That is exactly what he was hoping for.

162
00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:42.540
And then Greg said, wouldn't it be amazing if the boss was exactly like Michael Scott?

163
00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:47.720
So, the job is the same and then, like, the people are even sort of a step back.

164
00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.460
And that's when he was like, we should have Bob Odenkirk play that role.

165
00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:57.660
And I know I've told this story earlier on the podcast, but, you know, Bob Odenkirk was one of the finalists for Michael Scott.

166
00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:07.720
He tested for the role of Michael Scott, and because his wife, Naomi Odenkirk, is my longtime manager, she suggested that Bob and I prepare for our tests together.

167
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.580
Mostly because a lot of the audition was going to be improvisation, and I don't have that background, but Bob does.

168
00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:20.900
And he very generously got together with me and did some sort of Michael Scott-Pam improv together to prepare.

169
00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:22.940
Oh, my God, I was so starstruck.

170
00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.560
But that's where the idea of Bob playing alternate Michael Scott came from.

171
00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:28.820
In Greg's mind.

172
00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:33.080
Well, that's the brilliant thing about Greg, too, is that nothing is ever wasted.

173
00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:40.640
Like, the minute he sees something or thinks about it, he will just file that away in his brain and years later bring it back.

174
00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:41.400
That's right.

175
00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:48.020
Well, you know, we were pretty excited because Bob came in the studio to talk with us about his time on The Office.

176
00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.800
Yes, he did.

177
00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.600
It was such a fun interview.

178
00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:56.300
We're going to wait to air it until after we break down moving on part one and part two.

179
00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.460
But everybody just know that is coming up.

180
00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.020
Bob in the studio.

181
00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.160
Okay, moving on to Fast Fact 3.

182
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:07.740
Another big guest star, Mary Gillis as Aunt Shirley.

183
00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:10.540
This woman is such a brilliant character actress.

184
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.700
She was just fantastic.

185
00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:14.820
Fantastic.

186
00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.080
Talk about someone who could command a room.

187
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:18.260
Oh, yeah.

188
00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:18.580
Yeah.

189
00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:27.740
I mean, I remember her from her role in Midnight Run because even in, like, just small roles, she's one of those people who pop.

190
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.320
You guys might remember her.

191
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.100
She had a recurring role on Glee as Mrs. Hegberg.

192
00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.360
She's done a ton of television.

193
00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:40.820
Another one of my favorites is that she is on the Everybody Loves Raymond episode called Marie's Sculpture.

194
00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.120
Do you know that one?

195
00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:44.000
No, but now I'm curious.

196
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:50.100
Marie takes a sculpture class, and at the end of this big class—

197
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.740
Oh, I do kind of remember it now.

198
00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:56.680
They, like, unveil her sculpture, and it is just—it very much looks like a giant vagina.

199
00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.880
And nobody knows how to compliment it.

200
00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:02.020
It's very funny.

201
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.100
We got a fan question from Allison Jay in West Haven, Connecticut, who asked,

202
00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.020
Mary Gillis has always been one of my favorite guest stars on The Office.

203
00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.340
How did she get her role?

204
00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:14.560
Well, I've got some hot trivia on this.

205
00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.280
Did you know that Mary Gillis was not the first Aunt Shirley?

206
00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:19.460
No.

207
00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:20.800
She wasn't.

208
00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:22.260
Who was the first Aunt Shirley?

209
00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:23.040
Okay.

210
00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.820
This story has a lot of twists and turns.

211
00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:26.540
Oh.

212
00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:27.440
So here we go.

213
00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:27.780
Okay.

214
00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:34.620
So during this season nine, NBC was exploring the idea of an Office spinoff starring Rainn

215
00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:35.280
Wilson—

216
00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.340
This I know.

217
00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:37.780
—called The Farm.

218
00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:38.180
Yeah.

219
00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:44.880
Paul Lieberstein was the showrunner, and they shot an episode, and the plot of that episode

220
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.700
is that Dwight's Aunt Shirley has passed away and has left him, I guess, the farm?

221
00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:51.460
Yeah.

222
00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:51.960
Yeah.

223
00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.580
Well, they shot it long before we shot this episode.

224
00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.400
They shot it earlier in the year, and they cast a woman as Aunt Shirley.

225
00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:06.120
After it was finished, and they found out that the show wasn't going to go forward, they

226
00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.260
decided to make The Farm an Office episode.

227
00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:15.180
So they wrote—moving on, after they wrote The Farm as a way to precede The Farm.

228
00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.380
—to explain who Aunt Shirley is.

229
00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:18.080
Exactly.

230
00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.420
This character of Aunt Shirley was now like a big character.

231
00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:27.040
So they went out, and they cast Mary Ellis, and then they had to go back and reshoot the

232
00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.060
scenes in The Farm with Mary Gillis as Aunt Shirley.

233
00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:32.440
Did I explain that okay?

234
00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:33.400
You really did.

235
00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.180
And I think it also explains something.

236
00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:41.040
If you re-watch the show in order, like we're doing now, and we have watched The Farm.

237
00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:41.420
Yeah.

238
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.920
I remember watching it and thinking, why wasn't Angela invited to the funeral?

239
00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:46.640
I know!

240
00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:50.960
She bathed this woman and made her dinner and spent a whole day with her.

241
00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.200
Surely, she would want to attend the funeral.

242
00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.260
Of Aunt Shirley.

243
00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:56.100
Of Aunt Shirley.

244
00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.220
But we had filmed The Farm months before this.

245
00:11:59.360 --> 00:11:59.640
Yes.

246
00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:00.280
Uh-huh.

247
00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:00.940
Yeah.

248
00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.480
