Plus, she holds up her resume, and she says, my resume could fit on a Post-it note.

597
00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:11.030
Oh, lady, this was a fan mail flurry.

598
00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:12.150
I saw.

599
00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:15.490
People have lots of thoughts about Pam's resume.

600
00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:16.670
They really do.

601
00:27:16.990 --> 00:27:19.650
So I looked it up on Dunderpedia.

602
00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:23.410
They had the actual resume sort of, you know, laid out.

603
00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:31.990
And all she lists for her work experience is being a receptionist at Dunder Mifflin and being an office manager at Dunder Mifflin.

604
00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:36.490
For her education, she lists high school and Pratt.

605
00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:41.130
And then she says her interests are my children, art, and painting.

606
00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:42.950
Children, art, and painting.

607
00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:44.770
Let's break this down.

608
00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:45.990
Taryn G.

609
00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:52.050
from Camarillo, California said, we need to talk about Pam's resume being only two lines.

610
00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:59.210
Because she has actually been a receptionist, a salesperson at two different companies, and an office manager.

611
00:27:59.410 --> 00:28:01.450
She was an RA at Pratt.

612
00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:07.850
She could absolutely be filling an entire page with a description of duties of each of these jobs, her mural work.

613
00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.070
And then David H.

614
00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:17.330
from Preston, Lancashire, England, also would like to know why this resume does not include any of her sales experience.

615
00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:19.250
And Leanne J.

616
00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:28.470
from Burnbury, British Columbia, Canada says, in Season 8, Episode 5, Pam mentions that she worked at the Banshee Pub, where she saw the man in black.

617
00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:31.410
That's another job that could have helped bulk up her resume a bit.

618
00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:31.850
Yes.

619
00:28:32.050 --> 00:28:33.430
I mean, I remember that.

620
00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:40.550
When I was watching this, I thought, wait, remember Jim and Pam had this whole debate about ghosts because she worked at a restaurant and she saw a ghost?

621
00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:41.110
Yeah.

622
00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:43.790
She also went to college, and that's not listed.

623
00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:44.810
You know what?

624
00:28:44.810 --> 00:28:49.670
I had a theory from this Post-it resume is that does Pam even want this job?

625
00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:50.390
Right.

626
00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:52.410
It doesn't look like she put a lot of effort in this resume.

627
00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:53.470
Like from the beginning.

628
00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:54.990
She's kind of tanking it.

629
00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:57.310
I mean, maybe she's not aware that she's doing that.

630
00:28:57.310 --> 00:28:58.750
It's like a subconscious thing.

631
00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:05.370
Well, some people said maybe she doesn't want to talk about her time at Michael Scott Paper Company because it kind of wasn't really real.

632
00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:10.510
Maybe she doesn't want to talk about sales because she wasn't good at it and she doesn't want that kind of job again.

633
00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:11.930
I don't know.

634
00:29:12.370 --> 00:29:14.930
But you put all the things on a resume.

635
00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:16.130
You put all the things.

636
00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:20.450
Listen, as an aspiring actor, we know how to pad a resume.

637
00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:20.910
Yeah.

638
00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:21.950
Going back.

639
00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:28.730
Believe me, I'd had like two roles in some college shows, but it looked like I toured the world performing.

640
00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:30.790
Yeah.

641
00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:38.570
But you have to list like all of your skills in association with these jobs, and Pam doesn't do any of that.

642
00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:39.190
Right.

643
00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:47.090
Like she doesn't say like skills, managing people, takes initiative, any of the buzzwords.

644
00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:48.530
She didn't put a lot of thought into it.

645
00:29:48.830 --> 00:29:53.570
Well, listen, that wasn't the only controversy about Pam's resume.

646
00:29:54.790 --> 00:30:01.750
GGF from San Antonio, Texas said, I notice that Pam does not use the Oxford comma in her resume.

647
00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:07.110
What do you ladies have to say about this, and what are your opinions of the Oxford comma?

648
00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:08.390
Oh, no.

649
00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:09.410
I feel so bad.

650
00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:10.590
I don't know what it is.

651
00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:11.470
I had no idea what it was.

652
00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:12.330
I have to Google it.

653
00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:13.710
I Googled it for us.

654
00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:17.950
When I Googled it, here are some of the things that came up.

655
00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:20.090
You know how it gives you like suggested questions?

656
00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:20.630
Sure.

657
00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:24.090
It says, is it necessary to use the Oxford comma?

658
00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:27.110
Why is the Oxford comma controversial?

659
00:30:27.590 --> 00:30:29.010
Why is it controversial?

660
00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.850
Why don't people like the Oxford comma?

661
00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:32.770
Wow.

662
00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:33.350
Okay.

663
00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:36.670
I started with, what is an Oxford comma?

664
00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:37.950
I think that we should know.

665
00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:42.190
Gigi, I mean, I feel like we're about to have a real education here.

666
00:30:42.190 --> 00:30:43.190
Thanks to your question.

667
00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:43.870
All right.

668
00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:47.390
I had no idea what a hot-button topic this was in the world of grammar experts.

669
00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:55.350
But according to an article that I found on OxfordRoyale.com, they list the pros and cons of the Oxford comma.

670
00:30:55.730 --> 00:31:04.070
The article starts with a quote by Lynn Truss, who is the author of a book called Eats, Shoots, and Leaves, which is considered the punctuation Bible.

671
00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:05.070
Oh.

672
00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:05.570
Mm-hmm.

673
00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:08.330
And here's what she said about the Oxford comma.

674
00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:13.510
Quote, there are people who embrace the Oxford comma and people who don't.

675
00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:15.170
And I'll just say this.

676
00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:18.790
Never get between these people when a drink has been taken.

677
00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:20.110
Oh, my gosh.

678
00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:20.770
Wait.

679
00:31:20.990 --> 00:31:23.910
Is the Oxford comma, does it look like a semicolon?

680
00:31:24.170 --> 00:31:24.570
No, no, no.

681
00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:25.190
It's a comma.

682
00:31:25.430 --> 00:31:26.710
So here's how it works.

683
00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:38.230
If you're listing multiple things in a sentence, do you put a comma after every single one even before and, or do you leave the comma out when you get to the and?

684
00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:47.110
So, for example, this sentence, her favorite foods were chocolate comma, marshmallows comma, cake comma, and chicken.

685
00:31:56.090 --> 00:31:59.230
I was taught to put a comma before and.

686
00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:03.750
So there's a comma after each word listed even before the word and.

687
00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:05.170
It's very American of you.

688
00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:05.710
Okay.

689
00:32:05.710 --> 00:32:07.170
I was as well.

690
00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:12.990
I didn't realize that, you know, that would be considered controversial.

691
00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:18.450
But I guess some people feel like if you put the comma before and,

692
00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:29.850
that it's sort of like overly pendantic and you can sound pompous and hypercorrect because it is possible for people to know what you mean without using the comma.

693
00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:32.330
So you don't need to be all snooty about it.

694
00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:35.210
And is it more common in America than in other countries?

695
00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:36.290
It is.

696
00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:41.770
However, this article went through and even looked back at, like, historical texts.

697
00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:44.030
And here's what they found.

698
00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:48.950
That it is generally more common among American-style guides than British ones.

699
00:32:49.550 --> 00:32:53.950
The Times and The Economist, for instance, are both against its use.

700
00:32:54.110 --> 00:32:59.310
But many well-known American guides, such as the Chicago Manual of Style, advocate it.

701
00:32:59.310 --> 00:33:04.070
However, the Associated Press-style book does not use the Oxford comma.

702
00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:08.910
So the comma that comes before the and is known as the Oxford comma.

703
00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:09.470
Correct.

704
00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:10.970
I see now.

705
00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:14.250
And so the controversy is whether to use it or not.

706
00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:14.930
That's right.

707
00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:22.170
But French, German, and Italian do not use the Oxford comma, and there's no controversy about not using it.

708
00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:23.730
The Italians don't use it?

709
00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:24.970
Did I say Italians?

710
00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:25.490
Yes.

711
00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:26.750
That's right.

712
00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:28.190
The Italians.

713
00:33:28.630 --> 00:33:30.670
The Italians don't use it either.

714
00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:31.890
Either.

715
00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:32.990
What is happening?

716
00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:38.890
I want to just real quickly give an argument for the Oxford comma, if I may.

717
00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:39.370
Okay.

718
00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:41.150
If it won't just tick people off.

719
00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:48.870
So I gave you my sample sentence, which is like, her favorite foods were chocolate, marshmallows, cake, and chicken.

720
00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:49.510
Uh-huh.

721
00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:55.070
What if her favorite foods were chocolate, marshmallows, chicken, and waffles?

722
00:33:55.890 --> 00:34:02.250
Without the comma, it sounds like her favorite foods were chocolate, marshmallows, chicken, and waffles.

723
00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:10.250
or her favorite foods were chocolate, marshmallows, chicken, and waffles, not chicken and waffles.

724
00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:14.490
You really need the comma so that we know what her favorite foods were.

725
00:34:14.930 --> 00:34:16.930
I support this argument.

726
00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:18.070
You know what I'm saying?

727
00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:19.770
I do, sort of.

728
00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:24.930
To sum this up, I did look up should you use the Oxford comma on a resume,

729
00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:30.730
and it said while it's not mandatory, using it on a resume can enhance clarity,

730
00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:34.670
especially when listing multiple skills or job responsibilities.

731
00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:37.870
So maybe Pam should have used it.

732
00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:41.890
Well, Pam maybe should have just put more effort into this resume.

733
00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:43.250
There's that, too.

734
00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:44.190
All right.

735
00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:46.850
I hope you all enjoyed learning about the Oxford comma.

736
00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:48.150
I very much did.

737
00:34:48.170 --> 00:34:50.750
I now know it, and I'll never forget it.

738
00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:51.870
Thank you, Gigi.

739
00:34:52.930 --> 00:34:56.210
Well, let's take a break because when we come back,

740
00:34:56.370 --> 00:35:00.170
I had the table draft for this episode in my email.

